-- §15  What the layer dimension actually carries, and the order a fusion may be evaluated in.
# One row per (relation, kind): how many layers the relation reaches, of how many.
#
# Why a diagram of this corpus looks empty
#   The three relations a process notation would have drawn as arrows reach a handful of layers
#   each; `nameplate` and `slack` reach nearly all of them. The incidence side is sparse and the
#   magnitude side is total, and a notation can carry only the first. That is not a defect in
#   either.
#
# A `WHERE` on a relation that already exists
#   That is what most observations here should be: rank/incidence_reach.sqlc is the object, and
#   this restricts it to the two kinds and puts them beside each other, which is what an
#   observation is for.
-- rank/incidence_reach.sqlc, ordered so the two kinds sit beside each other.
SELECT c.relation  AS "relation!",
       c.kind      AS "kind!",
       c.reaches   AS "reaches!",
       c.of_layers AS "of!"
FROM (
    :compose(rank/incidence_reach.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.kind, c.reaches DESC, c.relation
